[SPEAKER_01]: need that. We have to be acclimatized when you fly over 5,000 meters. And to do that, it's simple. Just you have to go on the place or another place.

25:36.949 --> 25:48.021
[SPEAKER_01]: And you will sleep at the beginning at something like 3,000 meters. And the next day at 3,500, and the next day you climb more and more. And you

25:48.041 --> 25:58.280
[SPEAKER_01]: need just to spend time to have a good acclimatization. But you have to go step by step. I see. What I was wondering is that once you start flying,

25:58.480 --> 26:08.086
[SPEAKER_00]: let's say you go to Skardu and you go to Karimabad, as you said, or any of these locations in Peru that you were, do you start your expedition from

26:08.106 --> 26:17.012
[SPEAKER_00]: day one itself or do you go there and you fly around the area a little bit, you just check out how the topography is, how the conditions are? How do

26:17.032 --> 26:27.416
[SPEAKER_00]: you spend your first two, three days after reaching the place of action or the place of your project? It depends on the place. Because if I go, for

26:27.436 --> 26:38.971
[SPEAKER_01]: example, in Peru, I cross Peru from the north to the south. I was the first to do that and I'm alone to do that at this moment. It's a new place. So

26:39.231 --> 26:48.576
[SPEAKER_01]: nobody flew before at this place. So at the beginning, when you arrive, you have to check the weather for two or three days. You check in the

26:48.616 --> 26:56.097
[SPEAKER_01]: morning, in the middle of the day, at the end of the day. You understand the wind, you understand the weather and everything. And when you are

26:56.177 --> 27:08.920
[SPEAKER_01]: sure it's safe to fly, you can fly. But at the beginning, sometimes I stay two or three, four days, and I do nothing. Just a small flight, just to

27:09.000 --> 27:20.871
[SPEAKER_01]: check how it works. and when i'm sure i go inside but when you arrive from your country in a new country normally you have to read because you

27:20.931 --> 27:31.943
[SPEAKER_01]: travel a lot and everything it's good to have a rest and just take time to check everything and and after you can go inside Makes sense, makes

27:31.963 --> 27:45.512
[SPEAKER_00]: sense. I think now I would like to come about your 8,000 meter flights. So let's say once you start, you know, reaching, is it Skardu where you land

27:45.632 --> 27:50.135
[SPEAKER_00]: and eventually you take a trek to Karimabad? Is that how these flights happen in Pakistan?

27:52.737 --> 28:02.585
[SPEAKER_01]: Normally when we go into Pakistan, We go directly to Karimabad because Karimabad is a small city and we have easy takeoff at 20 minutes of walk.

28:02.886 --> 28:15.117
[SPEAKER_01]: It's very, very easy. If you go in Skardu, the Skardu area is forbidden to fly. So you have to go a little bit after Skardu and you need maybe 8 or 10

28:15.337 --> 28:27.768
[SPEAKER_01]: hours of walk to have a takeoff. So it can be very, very long. So normally we go in Karimabad because it's very, very easy. And we take time to fly in

28:27.828 --> 28:37.235
[SPEAKER_01]: Karimabad. And it's not difficult to move from Karimabad to Skardu in flights. It's not an easy flight, of course. It's a flight of 150

28:38.396 --> 28:50.512
[SPEAKER_01]: kilometers. And you have to fly over Spantik. Spantik is 7,000 meters. It's possible to pass the Spontik by a pass, but the pass is maybe 6,000

28:50.592 --> 28:58.715
[SPEAKER_01]: meters. It's very high. But it's not a difficult flight. It's a very, very beautiful commitment, of course, because you have a lot of

28:58.775 --> 29:06.637
[SPEAKER_01]: glaciers. And if you land, maybe you have to walk, I don't know, five days, something like that, to go outside the glacier. But it's not very

29:06.657 --> 29:16.038
[SPEAKER_01]: difficult, but a commitment. Right. I'm just wondering if you are a normal pilot who is usually used to flying in European or in

29:16.098 --> 29:23.581
[SPEAKER_00]: civilization, I would say. I would not just say European conditions. But if you're an average flyer who flies, let's say, 100 hours a year and

29:23.641 --> 29:37.349
[SPEAKER_00]: flies a sea glider and the usual altitude that you reach is 3,000, 4,000 meters maximum in normal places to fly. How different is flying in

29:37.429 --> 29:47.580
[SPEAKER_00]: places where you can reach 8,000 meters? Basically, where does the air start feeling different? Where do your wings start behaving

29:47.640 --> 29:58.989
[SPEAKER_00]: differently? How do How do thermals behave in this kind of thin air and collapses? And if you can just tell us, how does flying differentiate

29:59.349 --> 30:09.536
[SPEAKER_00]: from normal flying conditions that a lot of pilots fly in compared to this places like where the air is thin and the mountains are high? To

30:09.556 --> 30:21.304
[SPEAKER_01]: understand, we have to know that when you go in very high altitude, you have less and less air. So the air is very thin. And for your glider, you

30:21.344 --> 30:33.011
[SPEAKER_01]: have only one difference. This is the speed. If you fly in, I don't know, at 2000 meters, you will fly around 40 kilometers. If you fly around 8000

30:33.952 --> 30:48.357
[SPEAKER_01]: meters, you will fly at 60 kilometers. And if you are in the middle, it's 50 kilometers or something like that. But at 8,000 meters, it's 60. And

30:48.397 --> 30:55.870
[SPEAKER_01]: when you do that, you have to know everything is more fast. If you have a collapse, it's more fast because everything goes fast. It's same for

30:55.890 --> 31:06.100
[SPEAKER_01]: the landing, because if you fly at 40 km, if you want to land, you wait for 20 km, so you will land around 15 and 20 km. If you land at 5000 m, you will

31:06.140 --> 31:06.921
[SPEAKER_01]: fly around 50 km.

31:11.545 --> 31:23.235
[SPEAKER_01]: So when you brake for the landing, you will land at 25 km or 30 km. It's very fast, so it's difficult to run. So it's more difficult to land. And also

31:23.255 --> 31:27.559
[SPEAKER_01]: everything is fast. And for the other things, it's exactly the same.

31:30.475 --> 31:41.128
[SPEAKER_01]: The biggest difference is the speed. But when you fly in high altitude, you have another problem. It's because you don't have a lot of oxygen.

31:41.489 --> 31:55.739
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you don't have a lot of oxygen, your brand goes very slowly. True. So the glider goes very fast and you won't go very slowly. So it's not very

31:55.799 --> 32:03.882
[SPEAKER_01]: easy. It's why I propose some oxygen. For when the people don't know the high altitude, it's good to have oxygen because with oxygen, it's like

32:03.902 --> 32:14.746
[SPEAKER_01]: you're down of 2,000 meters. So if you fly at 6,000 meters, it's like to fly at 4,000 meters. So it's not difficult. It's better like this. I see.

32:15.387 --> 32:24.252
[SPEAKER_00]: Antoine, I want to ask you this question about death zone. This word is used very common in mountaineering and usually a lot of mountaineers

32:24.332 --> 32:33.117
[SPEAKER_00]: say above 8,000 it is known as death zone. Life does not sustain there very well and humans are not supposed to be there for long enough. But

32:33.217 --> 32:46.084
[SPEAKER_00]: when you push the limit in a cockpit like in a canopy like ours which is unpressurized, you're open, Is oxygen enough to keep you safe and alert

32:47.724 --> 32:57.188
[SPEAKER_00]: in those altitudes? Or can you only fly above 8000 for very limited time because of the dangers that come with it? And it is just that one or two

32:57.228 --> 33:07.109
[SPEAKER_00]: thermals and after that you have to descend. Like, how does life look in that zone when you're flying a paraglider, basically? In first, when

33:07.129 --> 33:15.033
[SPEAKER_01]: you fly at 8000 meters, it's not possible to fly, for example, for me for tomorrow because I need acclimatization. If you have a good

33:15.093 --> 33:26.659
[SPEAKER_01]: acclimatization, it's like the mountaineer, you can go at 8000 meters with no problem. And for the mountaineer, they can stay 3, 4, 5 days at

33:26.779 --> 33:37.804
[SPEAKER_01]: 8000 meters before to have some big problem. But with a paraglide, when you fly at 8,000 meters, it's maximum for one hour or two hours. If you

33:37.824 --> 33:48.368
[SPEAKER_01]: compare with a fourth day, it's like nothing. And another thing, when you fly, if you are bad or something like that, you can down in five

33:48.408 --> 33:58.690
[SPEAKER_01]: minutes. When you are in the mountain, you need maybe four, five, six hours to down. And in mountain, you have to walk. It's a big effort. It's

33:58.750 --> 34:11.057
[SPEAKER_01]: very, very hard. When you fly, you do nothing. You stay in your harness and just pilot with the bike. So you don't do a big effort. So you need less

34:11.117 --> 34:22.966
[SPEAKER_01]: oxygen than to do sport. So it's not very difficult to go at 8,000 meters. Okay, that's very encouraging to hear. I'm pretty sure if there are some

34:23.466 --> 34:30.773
[SPEAKER_00]: mountaineers listening to this, they want to try paragliding as well because it's easier and it gets you to 8,000 much quicker than what it

34:30.813 --> 34:42.165
[SPEAKER_00]: takes on foot. Just quickly before we move on, I wanted to touch upon the topic of thermals and scratching because I have heard... Sometimes Tom

34:42.205 --> 34:53.139
[SPEAKER_00]: de Dordodot or a lot of other pilots talking that thermals stop after a certain height and then you have to scratch. How do you find lift after a

34:53.199 --> 35:03.309
[SPEAKER_00]: certain height? Do they go all the way or does it become hard after a while? Like I was seeing a video of yours from Broad Peak where you were at

35:03.950 --> 35:15.437
[SPEAKER_00]: 7,600 meters without oxygen, if you can recall that one, and you were just drifting above and it seemed pretty windy at that height. So how is

35:15.497 --> 35:25.302
[SPEAKER_00]: basically lift and how does wind behave after a certain height once, yeah? Yeah. Yes, like everywhere, the thermals are different. So

35:25.322 --> 35:34.065
[SPEAKER_01]: sometimes in back history, you can climb at 6,000 meters. Sometimes it's 7,000, sometimes it's 8,000 meters with a thermal. My first flight

35:34.085 --> 35:36.986
[SPEAKER_01]: in 2016, I'm going at 8,100 meters with one thermal.

35:44.785 --> 35:57.133
[SPEAKER_01]: Wow. With different thermal, but only with thermal. And I had wind only after 8,100 meters. But my last world record at 8,400 meters, the

35:57.173 --> 36:11.460
[SPEAKER_01]: thermal stopped at 7,600 meters. And after this altitude, I used only the wind to climb. But every day is different. So we know what is the limit

36:11.481 --> 36:19.647
[SPEAKER_01]: of the thermal. What is the maximum altitude, something like that, nobody knows. So for sure, when it's windy, you have a wave with the wind

36:19.967 --> 36:32.957
[SPEAKER_01]: and you can climb in this wave. The last time I flew at 8,400, but I was in a wave and I climbed at 4 or 5 meters per second every time. But I decided to

36:32.997 --> 36:43.105
[SPEAKER_01]: stop because my goal was to fly at 8,400 to prepare my project. Okay, I'm at this altitude, so I go outside the wave. And I'm sure it was possible to

36:43.165 --> 36:53.293
[SPEAKER_01]: fly, sure, 8,600 and maybe 9,000 this day. But I was not ready, so I decided to stop. That sounds so exciting just to hear. I'm getting

36:53.353 --> 37:04.325
[SPEAKER_00]: goosebumps. You mentioned wave flying, and I've usually heard there's some bit of turbulence that is included. How does glider control look

37:04.385 --> 37:13.095
[SPEAKER_00]: like above a certain level? Because obviously, once you exit a terminal at a certain level, there is a bit of a shear factor. So is there a lot of

37:13.175 --> 37:24.432
[SPEAKER_00]: turbulence above a certain height or is it calm air or is it just as it is normal in flying? How does glider control differentiate from lower

37:24.492 --> 37:34.210
[SPEAKER_00]: altitudes to higher ones? For the turbulence, it's similar in Europe, exactly the same. When you go outside the thermal, you can have some

37:34.250 --> 37:44.316
[SPEAKER_01]: turbulence. But normally, everywhere in the world, you have a lot of turbulence when you have wind and thermal. Because the wind fights with

37:44.336 --> 37:55.679
[SPEAKER_01]: the thermal and creates some turbulence. But in Pakistan, it's a little bit different because not all the mountains, but a lot of mountains are

37:55.839 --> 38:06.445
[SPEAKER_01]: at 7,000 or 8,000 meters. So the wind is broken by the mountain. So you don't have wind. Under 7,500 meters, something like that, you don't

38:06.466 --> 38:15.558
[SPEAKER_01]: have wind. So you have only thermal, and when you have only some thermal and not wind, it's not turbulent. For me, we have more turbulence in

38:15.698 --> 38:18.422
[SPEAKER_01]: Europe than in Pakistan, under 7,500 meters.

38:21.263 --> 38:32.108
[SPEAKER_00]: Very interesting. And I think let's go to Peru for a little while, because I remember I read your Facebook post where you said after 7,800,

38:33.048 --> 38:45.794
[SPEAKER_00]: it was absolute nightmare until you reached 8,000 meters and the cloud just sucked you in. Can you help our listeners know what basically

38:45.834 --> 38:58.639
[SPEAKER_00]: happened and how did the temperatures reach minus 35? Like that... That seems scary. Can equipment failure happen when it is so cold? Minus 35

38:58.699 --> 39:08.493
[SPEAKER_00]: means everything is just freezing. Can the paraglider even handle that kind of temperatures at that height? Yes, in Peru, it was not a goal to fly

39:08.533 --> 39:23.098
[SPEAKER_01]: at 8,000 meters, but we were on a plateau at 5,000 meters, something like that. And when we flew, at this moment, we had a very, very strong wind

39:23.519 --> 39:37.749
[SPEAKER_01]: from the forest. It came from Brasilia to Peru. And in other way, we have a very strong wind from the plateau of Peru to go to the forest. And we have

39:37.829 --> 39:48.667
[SPEAKER_01]: in one, what is the name? Convergence. Convergence. It's a convergence, no? Yeah. Yes, we have a big convergence with these two

39:48.687 --> 40:04.241
[SPEAKER_01]: winds. And I managed to climb in this convergence. And I fly safely until 7,000 meters, maybe 7,300 meters, something like that. It was cool and

40:04.281 --> 40:24.451
[SPEAKER_01]: very fun. But in maybe 10 seconds, the thermal convergence will change because we have a big cloud. We will be created by the cold wind and the hot

40:24.491 --> 40:38.758
[SPEAKER_01]: wind. And in 10 seconds, I lose the view. I go inside the cloud. Normally, when you go inside a cloud, you are all the time on the border of the cloud.

40:39.058 --> 40:47.922
[SPEAKER_01]: So I climb a little bit like that on the border. But after 200 or 300 meters on the border of the cloud, I go inside because sometimes you go a little

40:47.942 --> 40:54.586
[SPEAKER_01]: bit inside because it moves very, very fast. So it's difficult to control. But when you go inside, you turn back and you go outside. Just

40:54.906 --> 41:05.274
[SPEAKER_01]: you need 10 seconds. But at this moment, I have a very big collapse when I go inside. I open my glider again and I lose the direction. I don't know if

41:05.335 --> 41:16.347
[SPEAKER_01]: I have to go in this direction or another one. And the air was very, very turbulent. So we have a lot of collapse every time. But I have a lot of

41:16.387 --> 41:27.714
[SPEAKER_01]: collapse, and it was not possible to have one way, because I go for 10 meters in this direction. I have a new collapse, and I turn on the right.

41:27.754 --> 41:38.621
[SPEAKER_01]: For example, I go on the right. It was not possible to have a direction, because my instrument, well, not with my instrument. I went to check my

41:38.661 --> 41:47.777
[SPEAKER_01]: instrument, but the turbulence was too strong. It was not possible to take one direction. And I don't have direction with the view because I

41:48.519 --> 41:58.889
[SPEAKER_01]: was in the cloud. So I don't have sun, I don't have the ground, I have nothing. And I have a lot of collapse. And with the collapse, I climb like

41:59.009 --> 42:11.419
[SPEAKER_01]: maybe six or seven meters per second. Wow. It was not a good moment. And when I arrived around 7,800 meters, the cloud was very, very cold. And my

42:11.479 --> 42:20.006
[SPEAKER_01]: glasses were totally frozen. So it was impossible to see something. My instrument is the same. The screen was totally frozen. So I don't have

42:20.066 --> 42:33.381
[SPEAKER_01]: information. And after a fight around 8,000 meters, I see the sun on the summit because this is the end of the cloud. So I managed to take one

42:33.421 --> 42:44.128
[SPEAKER_01]: direction. I take the direction of the sun and I go straight. Not in the good direction, but I go outside the cloud. And the temperature was at

42:44.608 --> 42:56.452
[SPEAKER_01]: minus 35 degrees. And at this moment, I was not ready to go at 8,000 meters. So I don't have the clothes for that. I have a small glove, and I

42:56.472 --> 43:09.756
[SPEAKER_01]: have nothing for that. So I was totally scared. I was afraid and totally frozen. My hand doesn't work. No, for me, it was not a good moment. I can

43:09.797 --> 43:19.422
[SPEAKER_00]: imagine. I'm so glad that you managed to come out safely. But I cannot help but imagine when the glider is collapsing again and again, is it

43:19.542 --> 43:28.127
[SPEAKER_00]: basically collapsing and still getting sucked by the cloud? Because is that what you're telling me, that six, seven meters it is going up even

43:28.187 --> 43:36.512
[SPEAKER_00]: when it's collapsing? So the suck in the cloud is so damn high and the temperature is falling and everything is happening. It must seem like

43:36.552 --> 43:48.280
[SPEAKER_00]: you're in a washing machine or something like that. at that kind of time. Yes, for me, I was totally afraid. I didn't want to do that. I did a small

43:48.320 --> 44:01.169
[SPEAKER_01]: mistake. I go inside and now I have to assume I have to do something, but I don't know what. For me, I was thinking maybe I'm dead because I can do

44:01.249 --> 44:17.836
[SPEAKER_01]: nothing. I'm like a paper in a storm. No, I can imagine. For me it was a very bad moment. Maybe the most bad for a while. Well, let's hope that never

44:17.876 --> 44:26.118
[SPEAKER_00]: happens. But just talking about equipment at minus 35, Antoine, do you have any idea if gliders or the paragliders, because they're just made

44:26.158 --> 44:35.122
[SPEAKER_00]: out of cloth and thin lines, Are they even designed to handle weather conditions like this? Is there a chance that lines could just freeze and

44:35.202 --> 44:42.945
[SPEAKER_00]: break off or something like that? Because I think deploying a reserve in such difficult conditions is pretty much difficult because obviously

44:42.985 --> 44:52.489
[SPEAKER_00]: a reserve is just going to get sucked in as well and get thrown around here and there. So is our aircraft, the paraglider, is it even designed to go

44:52.869 --> 45:03.526
[SPEAKER_00]: in such extreme conditions? Do you have any idea about that? Yes, for the materials, I speak at the beginning, before 2016, I speak a lot with the

45:03.566 --> 45:14.374
[SPEAKER_01]: manufacturer. Well, not with the manufacturer, most with the people who create the line, the people who create the tissue. I speak with them

45:15.094 --> 45:26.001
[SPEAKER_01]: and I ask if I go at minus 20 or more and everything, what's happened? And they tell me for the material, it's exactly the same. We have no

45:26.041 --> 45:38.487
[SPEAKER_01]: difference. It can be different if you go at, I don't know, maybe minus 200, something like that. But at the normal temperature, minus 50,

45:39.828 --> 45:52.337
[SPEAKER_01]: we don't have a problem. So I'm sure it's not a problem. But for me, the rescue is not one option. Because you tell that we are in like a storm or

45:52.858 --> 46:02.231
[SPEAKER_01]: with a very, very big thermal and with a parachute, maybe you climb more and it's very dangerous and you can't choose your direction. And

46:02.451 --> 46:11.597
[SPEAKER_01]: another thing also, When you fly in very high altitude, you have a big difference with a rescue. Because when you use a rescue, normally you

46:11.657 --> 46:23.344
[SPEAKER_01]: will land at something like minus five or six meters per second. It's very fast. But if you land at 5,000 meters, for example, Because the

46:23.364 --> 46:36.313
[SPEAKER_01]: ground is 5,000 meters. True. With the rescue, you will land at something minus 11, minus 13 kilometers, meters per second. What? So I

46:36.393 --> 46:46.765
[SPEAKER_01]: think you break your two legs. So it's not an option to use the rescue. For that, if you want to land with a rescue at 5,000 meters, you need a rescue

46:47.145 --> 46:58.578
[SPEAKER_01]: of a tandem, not a normal rescue. But in expedition, when you have a bag of 30 kilos, you can't have an additional 2 kilos for the rescue. Wow. In

46:58.698 --> 47:07.305
[SPEAKER_01]: fact, in all my expeditions, I have a rescue, but it's for nothing. It's just for my mind because, okay, if I have a problem, I have a rescue. But in

47:07.365 --> 47:18.195
[SPEAKER_01]: fact, I can't use the rescue. Yeah, I can imagine coming down at minus 10 or anything beyond is a sure shot injury. But anyways, let's hope that

47:18.435 --> 47:26.644
[SPEAKER_00]: situation never comes for anyone. But okay, now coming to the point where you have this 8000paragliding.com

47:30.448 --> 47:40.091
[SPEAKER_00]: which allow people to join you in these amazing flights that you are making it happen. I just want to bring out to our listeners, if there is

47:40.131 --> 47:48.093
[SPEAKER_00]: somebody who is interested in joining this, what kind of background should they have in paragliding? Like, what kind of a pilot should they

47:48.133 --> 48:00.363
[SPEAKER_00]: be to be able to join you safely and make it a memorable experience above 8,000 meters? What kind of pilot are you looking for? For me, I ask people

48:00.723 --> 48:09.649
[SPEAKER_01]: to fly often 100 kilometers in the Alps, for example, not in the flatland. For the flatland, it's more like 200 kilometers. Because if

48:09.669 --> 48:21.757
[SPEAKER_01]: you fly often 100 kilometers in the Alps, you will cross all the conditions that you can find in Pakistan. Because for me, in Pakistan,

48:21.837 --> 48:31.942
[SPEAKER_01]: it's more safe than in the Alps. For sure, it's more safe. But sometimes you can have a storm, you can have something. So it's good to have a good

48:32.002 --> 48:46.314
[SPEAKER_01]: level. In Pakistan, what is difficult is to fight with the altitude. So it's good to have a better level than you need to fight with the altitude.

48:47.875 --> 48:59.198
[SPEAKER_01]: But I'm sure it's more safe to fly in Pakistan than in the Alps for the turbulence. But for flying, it's difficult also with altitude. So it's

48:59.218 --> 49:09.766
[SPEAKER_01]: different. I see. But anybody who usually is able to navigate 100 kilometers in Alps is kind of ready to take on the conditions in Pakistan

49:09.846 --> 49:21.930
[SPEAKER_00]: and also chase the glory above 8,000 with you so far. Yes, yes, it's possible. But it's all the time the same. This is the level to enter in the

49:21.950 --> 49:32.035
[SPEAKER_01]: expedition. After, if you want to break the world record, if you want to do a flight of 200 kilometers in Pakistan or 300, because last year Iran

49:32.055 --> 49:45.160
[SPEAKER_01]: did 300 kilometers in Pakistan, you need a better level. with 100 km in the Alps. This is the level to enter the expedition and try to go to fly at

49:45.600 --> 49:55.847
[SPEAKER_01]: 7000 or something like that. So nice. So nice to hear that. I just want to come to basically the type of glider. These days we see a lot of

49:55.907 --> 50:03.712
[SPEAKER_00]: two-liners coming on the market and a lot of them are like they're very collapse resistant. They don't collapse that easily and they are much

50:03.752 --> 50:15.962
[SPEAKER_00]: more efficient than three-liners. Is there a difference between how a two-liner and three-liner behaves in these expeditions? And is there

50:16.142 --> 50:24.429
[SPEAKER_00]: any preferred recommendation that you should have a three- liner or a two-liner to be on a safer side or on a more easier side? Let me put it

50:24.469 --> 50:38.656
[SPEAKER_00]: across that way. Yes, for me, in fact, we have three kinds of gliders. You have the glider with three lines, the glider with two lines. But in the

50:38.836 --> 50:50.564
[SPEAKER_01]: two lines, you have two different kinds of gliders. You have the glider like the Zeno or Enzo. It's very high level. And the glider like Zeolite,

50:51.084 --> 51:02.956
[SPEAKER_01]: it's another level. It's intermediate. And it's very different. A lot of people who flow a lot with a Zeolite take, for example, a Zeno, and

51:03.036 --> 51:10.485
[SPEAKER_01]: after two weeks, they stop because it's too hard. You have a big difference between a Zeolite and a Zeno. But you don't have a big

51:10.506 --> 51:20.314
[SPEAKER_01]: difference between a three-liner and a Zeolite, for example. Okay. So we have to understand, for me, we have three kinds of gliders. But in all

51:20.374 --> 51:32.239
[SPEAKER_01]: cases, when you go in Pakistan, for example, you need to have an easy level. For me, I fly all the time with a Zeno in France or in the Alps or

51:32.259 --> 51:40.223
